.TH CWE-521 7 "2026-11-15" "CWE Top 25 (2025)" "Software Weakness Reference"
.SH NAME
CWE\-521 \- Weak Password Requirements
.SH RANK
KEV\-referenced weakness. Not part of the 2025 CWE Top 25; documented here because it appears in the CISA KEV catalog.
.SH CLASSIFICATION
Class: KEV\-referenced weakness.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The product does not require that users should have strong passwords, which makes it easier for attackers to compromise user accounts. Authentication mechanisms often rely on a memorized secret (also known as a password) to provide an assertion of identity for a user of a system. It is therefore important that this password be of sufficient complexity and impractical for an adversary to guess. The specific requirements around how complex a password needs to be depends on the type of system being protected. Selecting the correct password requirements and enforcing them through implementation are critical to the overall success of the authentication mechanism.
.SH KNOWN EXPLOITED VULNERABILITIES
CISA KEV catalog (as of 2026-11-15): \fB1\fR exploited CVE mapped to this weakness. Each entry has the catalog write\-up of how it was used in the wild (not a runnable exploit) and the required remediation. Code\-level vulnerable\->fixed pairs, when curated, are under \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SS CVE\-2019\-18988
TeamViewer Desktop, added 2021\-11\-03.
.PP
TeamViewer Desktop Bypass Remote Login Vulnerability.
.PP
\fIHow it is exploited (CISA):\fR
TeamViewer Desktop allows for bypass of remote\-login access control because the same AES key is used for different customers' installations. If an attacker were to know this key, they could decrypt protected information stored in registry or configuration files or decryption of the Unattended Access password to the system (which allows for remote login to the system).
.PP
\fIRemediation:\fR
Apply updates per vendor instructions.
 Due 2022\-05\-03.
.PP
